.TH SVCCTL 1
.SH NAME
svcctl \- inspect and control svcd services
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B svcctl
[
.B status
]
.br
.B svcctl
.BI start " name"
.br
.B svcctl
.BI stop " name"
.br
.B svcctl
.BI restart " name"
.br
.B svcctl
.B log
.I name
.br
.B svcctl
.B stopall
.br
.B svcctl
.B startall
.br
.B svcctl
.B ctl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Svcctl
shows the state of the services managed by
.IR svcd (8)
and commands changes.
.PP
With no arguments, or with
.BR status ,
it prints
.B /tmp/svcd/status
one line per service:
.IP
.EX
cpu running pid=31 restarts=0
tick backoff pid=0 restarts=4
.EE
.PP
A state of
.B running
means the service pid is alive.
.B running(group)
means the service leader exited but its process group is still
serving,
.I listen (1)
being the usual cause.
.B backoff
means the service exited and is waiting to be restarted;
.B restarts
counts the restarts since
.I svcd
started.
.PP
The
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR restart ,
.B stopall
and
.B startall
commands are written to
.BR /tmp/svcd/ctl .
A stopped service stays stopped until it is started again or
.I svcd
restarts; the stop is not recorded anywhere that survives
.IR svcd .
.PP
.B svcctl log
.I name
prints that service's output, and
.B svcctl ctl
prints the
.I svcd
event log.
.SH FILES
.TF /tmp/svcd/status
.TP
.B /tmp/svcd/status
service state
.TP
.B /tmp/svcd/ctl
control file
.TP
.B /tmp/svcd/log
.I svcd
events
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/svcctl.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR svcd (8)
